🔹 1. git init

Usage:

git init


What it does:
Initializes a new Git repository in your current directory. It creates a .git folder.

When to use:
When starting a new project and want to track it using Git.

🔹 2. git clone

Usage:

git clone <repository_url>


What it does:
Downloads a copy of a remote repository to your local system.

Example:

git clone https://github.com/user/project.git

🔹 3. git status

Usage:

git status


What it does:
Shows the current state of the working directory:

Modified files

New files

Staged vs unstaged changes

🔹 4. git add

Usage:

git add <file>
git add .



What it does:
Moves changes from working directory → staging area.

Examples:

git add file.txt     # Adds one file
git add .            # Adds all files

🔹 5. git commit

Usage:

git commit -m "Your message"


What it does:
Saves staged changes to the local repository with a message.

Example:

git commit -m "Fixed login bug"

🔹 6. git push

Usage:

git push origin <branch>


What it does:
Uploads your local commits to the remote repository.

Example:

git push origin main

🔹 7. git pull

Usage:

git pull


What it does:
Fetches and merges changes from the remote repo into your local branch.

Equivalent to:

git fetch + git merge

🔹 8. git fetch

Usage:

git fetch


What it does:
Downloads changes from remote but DOES NOT merge automatically.

🔹 9. git branch
➤ List branches
git branch

➤ Create branch
git branch <branch-name>

➤ Delete branch
git branch -d <branch-name>

🔹 10. git checkout
➤ Switch branches
git checkout <branch-name>

➤ Create + switch
git checkout -b <branch-name>

🔹 11. git merge

Usage:

git merge <branch>


What it does:
Merges another branch into your current branch.

Example:

git merge dev

🔹 12. git log

Usage:

git log


What it does:
Shows commit history.

Shorter format:
git log --oneline

🔹 13. git reset
➤ Reset staged file (soft reset)
git reset <file>

➤ Undo commit but keep changes
git reset --soft HEAD~1

➤ Undo commit & remove changes
git reset --hard HEAD~1

🔹 14. git revert

Usage:

git revert <commit-id>


What it does:
Creates a NEW commit that reverses an old commit.

Safer than reset.

🔹 15. git stash
➤ Save changes temporarily
git stash

➤ View stashes
git stash list

➤ Apply stash
git stash apply

🔹 16. git remote
➤ List remotes
git remote -v

➤ Add remote
git remote add origin <url>

🔹 17. git diff

Usage:

git diff              # unstaged changes
git diff --staged     # staged changes


Shows line-by-line differences.

🔹 18. git rm

Usage:

git rm <file>


Deletes a file and stages the deletion.

🔹 19. git tag
➤ Create tag
git tag v1.0

➤ Push tag
git push origin v1.0

🔹 20. git config
➤ Set username and email
git config --global user.name "Nikhil"
git config --global user.email "nikhil@example.com"

➤ Check config
git config --list

2. Advanced Commit & Version History Commands
Show complete commit history (graph format)
git log --oneline --graph --decorate --all

Search commits using keywords
git log --all --grep="bug fix"

Show commit that modified a specific file
git log --follow <filename>

Show what changed in the last commit
git show
git show <commit-id>

Show file content from a specific version
git show <commit-id>:<path/file.ext>

🔁 3. Version Control & Reverting to Previous Versions
Reset to a previous version (soft – keep changes staged)
git reset --soft <commit-id>

Reset to previous version (mixed – keep files but unstage)
git reset <commit-id>

Reset to previous version (hard – delete working changes)
git reset --hard <commit-id>

Undo a specific commit without deleting history
git revert <commit-id>

Create a new commit from an old version
git checkout <commit-id>


Then create a branch:

git checkout -b hotfix-from-old-version

🌿 4. Advanced Branching & Release Versioning
Create a branch for a new release
git checkout -b release/v2.0

Tag the current version
git tag v2.1

Tag with message (annotated tag – recommended)
git tag -a v2.1 -m "Release version 2.1"

Push tags to remote
git push origin --tags

Delete a tag
git tag -d v2.1
git push origin :refs/tags/v2.1

🧩 5. Stash & Version Safe-Keeping (Advanced)
Save changes with message
git stash push -m "WIP: implementing login"

List stashes
git stash list

Apply a specific stash
git stash apply stash@{2}

Apply and delete stash
git stash pop

Create a stash branch
git stash branch new-feature stash@{0}

🧠 6. Debugging Version Issues
Find which commit broke something (binary search)
git bisect start
git bisect bad        # current commit is bad
git bisect good <commit-id>


Git will help you find the exact commit that introduced the bug.

⚠️ 7. Rewrite History (Advanced, Dangerous)

Only use before pushing to shared branch.

Interactive rebase (combine, edit, drop commits)
git rebase -i HEAD~10

Change commit message of last commit
git commit --amend

Change author of a commit
git commit --amend --author="Nikhil <email@domain.com>"

Squash multiple commits

Inside rebase (pick → squash):

git rebase -i HEAD~5

🌐 8. Working With Remotes (Version Control with Repo)
Show all remote URLs
git remote -v

Change remote repo URL
git remote set-url origin <new-url>

Fetch from remote without merging
git fetch origin

View difference between your branch vs remote
git diff origin/main

🆚 9. Compare Versions (Diff Between Two Tags/Commits)
Compare two commits
git diff <commit1> <commit2>

Compare two versions (tags)
git diff v1.0 v2.0

Compare branch vs main
git diff main..feature/login

🚚 10. Cherry Pick (Bring specific version to another branch)
Copy a specific commit to another branch
git checkout main
git cherry-pick <commit-id>

Cherry pick a range of commits
git cherry-pick <commit1>^..<commit2>

📦 11. Clean Working Directory
Remove untracked files
git clean -f

Remove untracked files + folders
git clean -fd